// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Are password managers safe to use?',
    answer: '<p><strong>Yes, password managers are much safer than the alternative.</strong> Reputable password managers use AES-256 encryption (the same standard used by governments and banks) to protect your vault. Your master password never leaves your device, and the encrypted vault is useless without it.</p><p>The real risk is using weak, reused passwords—which is what happens without a password manager. Studies show the average person reuses passwords across 5+ accounts. One breach exposes them all. A password manager eliminates this risk entirely.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if my password manager gets hacked?',
    answer: '<p>Reputable password managers use <strong>zero-knowledge architecture</strong>, meaning even they cannot access your passwords. If their servers are breached, attackers only get encrypted data that\'s useless without your master password.</p><p>This happened to LastPass in 2022—encrypted vaults were stolen, but users with strong master passwords remained protected. The lesson: use a strong, unique master password (16+ characters), and enable two-factor authentication.</p>'
  },
