  <!-- Security Best Practices -->
  <h2 id="security-tips">Security Best Practices for Remote Workers</h2>
  <p>
    A password manager is your foundation, but complete security requires good habits:
  </p>

  <h3>Create a Strong Master Password</h3>
  <p>
    Your master password protects everything. Make it at least 16 characters using a passphrase method: string together
    random words with numbers and symbols. "Purple-elephant-runs-47-miles!" is strong and memorable.
  </p>

  <h3>Enable Two-Factor Authentication (2FA)</h3>
  <p>
    Always enable 2FA on your password manager. Use an authenticator app (not SMS) or a hardware key like YubiKey for
    maximum security. This protects you even if your master password is compromised.
  </p>

  <h3>Use Unique Passwords Everywhere</h3>
  <p>
    Let your password manager generate a unique random password for every account. 20+ characters, mixed case, numbers,
    and symbols. You never need to type them—the manager fills them in.
  </p>

  <h3>Review Password Health Regularly</h3>
  <p>
    Monthly, check your password manager's security dashboard. Fix weak passwords, update old ones, and remove duplicate
    entries. Most breaches could be prevented by addressing flagged issues.
  </p>

  <h3>Set Up Emergency Access</h3>
  <p>
    Designate a trusted person who can request access to your vault after a waiting period. Essential for solo remote
    workers—if something happens to you, critical accounts remain accessible.
  </p>

  <h3>Pair with a VPN</h3>
  <p>
    On public WiFi, use a <a href="/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads">VPN</a> alongside your password manager. The manager
    protects your credentials; the VPN protects your connection. Together, they provide comprehensive protection for
    remote work.
  </p>

